 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** $O(1)$ per evaluation, requiring 7 evaluations of the underlying noise function (1 at center, 6 for neighbors) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Minimal, as it is an analytical function. If pre-computed as a texture, memory depends on resolution and channel count (e.g., 3 channels for full 3D curl, 2 for simplified 2D) [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Guarantees divergence-free flow, resulting in visually smooth and physically plausible fluid-like motion without simulation [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** The approximation of derivatives via finite differences can introduce artifacts if $h$ is too large or too small (precision issues) [S1, unofficial]. The resulting flow is static unless time is varied [S2].
-*   **Use Cases:** Particle advection, cloud movement, smoke visualization [S2].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The method requires evaluating the underlying noise function 4-5 times per query (depending on dimensionality). Noise evaluation is typically $O(1)$ per point. Memory usage is minimal if computed on-the-fly; if precomputed as a texture, it scales with the resolution of the noise grid [S1, unofficial][S2].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+Performance depends heavily on the underlying noise implementation. GPU-based implementations can compute curl noise per-pixel or per-vertex efficiently [S2].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+-   **Strengths:** Guarantees divergence-free flow, which prevents particles from clumping or diverging unnaturally. It is computationally cheaper than full fluid simulation [S2].
+-   **Limitations:** The flow is not physically accurate in terms of pressure or viscosity. The choice of $h$ affects the smoothness and scale of the flow features [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+Use for particle advection, procedural cloud textures, or any scenario requiring smooth, organic, divergence-free motion without the cost of simulation [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Curl noise was introduced as a method to generate divergence-free vector fields for visual effects, particularly in computer graphics for simulating fluid-like motion [S2]. It leverages the mathematical property that the curl of any vector field is divergence-free [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique is often implemented using Perlin or Simplex noise as the base scalar field [S2].
+Curl noise was introduced as a technique to generate divergence-free vector fields from scalar noise functions. It is widely used in computer graphics for simulating natural phenomena like smoke, clouds, and water surfaces where mass conservation (divergence-free property) is visually important [S2]. The method leverages the fact that the curl of any vector field is divergence-free ($\nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) = 0$) [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
